<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Summarize the main points of various non-fiction texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of career-focused texts (e.g., "Read the job description and find the key duties."). Can summarize main points of texts related to career aspirations (e.g., "List two skills needed to be a doctor."). Can distinguish and correctly pronounce minimal pairs, demonstrating improved phonemic discrimination (e.g., /ʃ/ in "ship" versus /tʃ/ in "chip," and /dʒ/ in "joke" versus /tʃ/ in "choke").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 40%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 45%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various professionals (a doctor, a lawyer, a teacher, an engineer) on the board or projector. Students identify the careers. The teacher then asks students to share which career they might be interested in. (e.g., "What do you want to be in the future?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing future intentions using target grammar. "I want to be a doctor. I will need to study hard." "I would like to become a teacher because I like helping others." The teacher writes key vocabulary (doctor, teacher, professional, goals, plan) on the board and models their pronunciation clearly, especially focusing on the /ʊ/ sound in "would" and /ɪ/ sound in "will."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you want to be a doctor in the past, present, or future?" (Future)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I will study medicine,' am I studying now or later?" (Later)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short non-fiction text (e.g., a simplified article or infographic description) titled "Your Path to Becoming a Doctor." The text describes the steps, qualifications, and training required for this career. It includes target vocabulary like <i>doctor, plan, train, achieve, qualifications, internship, professional, succeed, practice</i>. It also incorporates grammar: "I will need to...", "want to + infinitive", and "modals for intention/suggestions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Summarize the main points of various non-fiction texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the text individually and answer: "What is the main topic of this text?" and "What is the text trying to teach you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements related to the text and mark them as True or False (e.g., "To become a doctor, you only need to finish high school.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match steps (e.g., "Go to university") with descriptions of what you *will need to do* at each stage (e.g., "I will need to study science subjects").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice reading for specific information, summarizing, and identifying text structure. Grammar: Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), "Want to + infinitive." Vocabulary: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, professional, skilled, important, successfully, professionally. Phonemic Awareness: Minimal pairs /ʃ/ vs /tʃ/ and /dʒ/ vs /tʃ/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Text Structure Analysis (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the reading text, leaving blanks for key structural words or vocabulary. Students read the text again to complete the sentences (e.g., "To ______ your dream, you ______ need to get the right _______."). Focus on nouns: <i>career, goals, qualifications</i>, and verbs: <i>achieve, train, plan, succeed</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Summarizing Main Points (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in small groups. Each group reads a specific paragraph from the "Path to Becoming a Doctor" text. They identify 2-3 main points and write them down using "I will need to..." or "One must/should...". For example, "First, I will need to finish high school." "Then, I should apply for university to train professionally."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pronunciation Spot & Peer Check (Pair Interaction):</b> The teacher presents a list of words from the text (or related to the theme) that include the target minimal pair sounds: /ʃ/ (e.g., "professional," "finish," "wish") and /tʃ/ (e.g., "achieve," "teacher," "future," "challenge"). Also, words with /dʒ/ (e.g., "job," "major," "manage") vs /tʃ/ (e.g., "choke" - less directly from text but for discrimination). Students read the words aloud to their partner, who listens for correct pronunciation and gives feedback. They then practice saying phrases like "I will finish my challenge" or "My future job is important."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Information Exchange. Students work in groups of three. Each student in the group receives a different short non-fiction text (e.g., "How to Become a Lawyer," "Steps to Becoming a Teacher," "Qualifications for an Engineer"). These texts are similar in structure to the "Doctor" text from the Presentation stage, using the same core vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Each student silently reads their assigned text, focusing on its structure and identifying the main steps, qualifications, and goals for that career.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students then take turns summarizing their text to their group members, explaining the career path. They use phrases like, "To become a [lawyer], you will need to [go to law school]," "I want to [achieve] my goals [professionally]," and answer Wh-questions from their peers like, "What qualifications do you need?" or "What training is important?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The group collaborates to create a brief comparative chart or a mind map on a large sheet of paper, outlining the similarities and differences in the career paths they read about (e.g., "All careers need a good plan," "Some need internships, others don't"). This encourages participation in group activities focused on educational and career planning, and making suggestions for improvement.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage and review of the group's comparative chart/mind map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their group's comparative chart or mind map from the Performance stage. Additionally, the teacher asks each student to write one short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing the career path of one of the careers discussed in their group, applying the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation and Written Summary):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Analyzes the structure of career-focused texts (identifies key duties, steps).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes main points of texts related to career aspirations (clear main ideas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>qualifications, internship, professional</i>) correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies future tense and "want to + infinitive" accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key words, including minimal pairs (observed during group interaction).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new things you learned about future careers today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you in reading and understanding texts about career paths? (1=Not confident, 5=Very confident)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and Teacher reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's reading activities helped you understand career paths the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one career you are now more interested in learning about, and what is one thing you will need to do to achieve it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today, the students successfully engaged with the reading tasks and were able to extract key information from the career texts. The group activity encouraged collaborative reading and summarization. The pronunciation practice with minimal pairs was a good addition, though some students still need more practice differentiating /ʃ/ and /tʃ/. I noticed some students still rely heavily on direct translation rather than inferring meaning. For the next lesson, I will need to scaffold more speaking opportunities to allow students to articulate their summaries and career plans more fluidly, reinforcing the vocabulary and grammar used today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The reading comprehension and summarization practiced today will directly prepare students for actively speaking and interacting about their career aspirations and plans in the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction). They will use the facts and vocabulary learned from the texts to discuss their own future goals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>